# S12.1 r3 Worktree-handle false positive — 2026-10-01

## Observed failure

The sole r3 controller invocation returned
`S12_1_RECOVERY_GIT_ACTIVE_RED` before backup, deployment-attempt marker,
repository synchronization, runtime installation or provider acceptance.
Application and Control remained clean on their pre-deployment commits.

## Reproduction from canonical r3 source

`_serialized_repository_recovery()` passed `APPLICATION_ROOT` and
`CONTROL_ROOT` directly to `_active_recovery_git_handle_count()`. That helper
recursively materialized every inode below each supplied path. The four healthy
S7/S8/S10 units use the Application Worktree as `WorkingDirectory`; their cwd,
ordinary file and `.venv` handles were therefore counted as Git-recovery
handles even though neither `.git` tree was retained.

## Secondary permission finding

The r3 parent/root locks changed the shared repository parent and checkout
roots to `root:root` mode `0500`. That prevented new namespace writers but also
removed traversal from the `chatops` service identity. Narrowing only the
handle scanner would therefore have left an avoidable live-service risk.

## Decision

`RECOVERY_FIX_METADATA_SCOPING_SUFFICIENT`.

The pinned Application delta changes only S12/Yoti modules, tests,
documentation and packaging metadata; the active S7/S8/S10 entrypoints do not
import those modules. The existing `.venv` is an ignored preserved path and is
not replaced by canonical Git synchronization. For this exact frozen release,
healthy services may remain running while Git metadata is isolated and the
reviewed tracked delta is applied.

The r4 contract:

1. detects actual Git processes using cwd, chained `-C`, explicit Git paths and
   Git environment selectors;
2. scans cwd, fds and mappings only against `.git` and
   `.git.s12-1-recovery` inode trees;
3. removes namespace write permission from the shared parent and repository
   roots while retaining the recorded group's read/traverse classes;
4. fingerprints the complete Git-metadata tree, installs a kernel mutation
   watch, requires a matching post-install fingerprint and only then performs
   the first process scan;
5. locks real `.git` metadata to `root:root` mode `0700`, then rejects any
   fingerprint drift or queued mutation event from even an already-exited
   writer before exchange;
6. repeats writer and metadata-handle checks before atomic exchange;
7. atomically exchanges `.git` with a root-owned mode-`000` guard while the
   mutation watch remains active;
8. checks both guards and quarantined metadata before root-only Git use;
9. separately resolves regular index entries and rejects write-capable FDs,
   writable shared mappings and write-upgradeable shared mappings while still
   allowing read-only Worktree users and private copy-on-write mappings;
10. repeats that resource-based writer check through the transition and against
    the final tracked set, then requires a fresh clean identity and a second
    final handle scan before barrier removal;
11. durably journals and installs a tracked-path write barrier that preserves
    read/traversal access but prevents new owner writes through Git-metadata
    teardown;
12. revalidates the clean identity under that barrier after canonical `.git`
    returns, then restores exact recorded ownership/modes as the explicit
    writer-release point.

There is no process-name allowlist. A Git process under a product-service
identity remains RED.

## Server observation

Both canonical `.git` directories were normal and both recovery directories
were absent after the r3 failure. The private journal could not be
distinguished as absent versus root-private from the unprivileged read-only
inspection. It must be classified at the single authenticated server
checkpoint and, if present, recovered only through the reviewed controller.

## Required acceptance

Linux regression must retain four live-shaped Worktree users while the barrier
is installed, reject actual Git writers and Git/recovery metadata handles,
exercise the new-writer race, recover the orphan journal canonically, preserve
durability, and keep S11/Public WMS unchanged.
